1
00:02:14,429 --> 00:02:15,932
<v WOMAN> Hello, how may I help you?


2
00:02:15,932 --> 00:02:29,947
<v MAN>Well, I’ve been seeing these yellow boxes in front of a lot of houses in my neighbourhood. I just wondered what they were for. I noticed your phone number on all of them, so I called. Could you tell me about your business?

3
00:02:29,947 --> 00:02:40,185
<v WOMAN> We do do recycling, but we’re not a business. Gaea’s Guardians is a non-profit group. We encourage recycling as a way of protecting the environment.

4
00:02:40,185 --> 00:02:46,921
<v MAN>I don’t know. I mean it is a good idea. But I really don’t read the newspaper every day or anything.

5
00:02:46,921 --> 00:02:53,342
<v WOMAN> And we don’t come collect newspaper every day! In fact, we only do pickups every other week.

6
00:02:53,342 --> 00:03:02,502
<v MAN>Oh, well then maybe I could help. I mean in my neighbourhood, there’s too much rubbish lying around everywhere. I’d like to help out, I guess.

7
00:03:02,502 --> 00:03:10,374
<v WOMAN> That’s great, sir. You’re doing the right thing. OK, I need to get your contact information. What is your name, please?

8
00:03:10,374 --> 00:03:11,979
<v MAN>Peter Wisrough..

9
00:03:11,979 --> 00:03:18,153
<v WOMAN> Peter... how do you spell your last name? W-I-S-R-O-W?

10
00:03:18,153 --> 00:03:23,756
<v MAN>No, actually it’s W-I-S-R-O-U-G-H.

11
00:03:23,756 --> 00:03:26,556
<v WOMAN> Oh, I’m sorry. I’m a terrible speller.

12
00:03:26,556 --> 00:03:31,232
<v MAN>You’re a good speller. It’s just that my family are terrible pronouncers!

13
00:03:31,232 --> 00:03:35,380
<v WOMAN> You’re quite a card, Peter! OK, now what’s your address?

14
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:42,024
<v MAN>No. 168 Bridge Road. That’s here in London. How about if I have any questions?

15
00:03:42,024 --> 00:03:45,097
<v WOMAN> I'm sending you a copy of our booklet, too.

16
00:03:45,097 --> 00:04:01,767
<v WOMAN>The booklet has our phone number and email address helpline@blackcat.com. That's H-E-L-P-L-I-N-E at B-L-A-C-K-C-A-T dot com.

17
00:04:01,767 --> 00:04:05,772
<v WOMAN> But I nearly forgot to ask, what’s your postcode?

18
00:04:05,762 --> 00:04:08,793
<v MAN>BS97PU

19
00:04:08,793 --> 00:04:11,812
<v WOMAN> PS97BU?

20
00:04:11,812 --> 00:04:18,655
<v MAN>No. That’s B as in “boy"-S-9-7-P as in “Peter”-U.

21
00:04:55,482 --> 00:04:59,969
<v MAN>So, I’m guessing those yellow boxes I saw are for recycled newspapers?

22
00:04:59,969 --> 00:05:03,241
<v WOMAN> Yes, that is correct, and it’s free of charge.

23
00:05:03,241 --> 00:05:08,821
<v MAN>Wow, that’s good news! Do you recycle anything besides newspaper? 

24
00:05:08,821 --> 00:05:13,526
<v WOMAN>Oh yes, we recycle most everything. Glass, plastic, paper...

25
00:05:13,526 --> 00:05:18,768
<v MAN>Oh, so I can put like glass and plastic bottles in the box?

26
00:05:18,768 --> 00:05:23,798
<v WOMAN>Sorry again. Things like that you have to bring to our collection centre. 

27
00:05:23,798 --> 00:05:26,110
<v MAN>And where is that?

28
00:05:26,110 --> 00:05:32,113
<v WOMAN>Our main centre isn’t that far from you. It’s actually right on the East Side of Central Park

29
00:05:32,113 --> 00:05:34,105
<v MAN>That new blue building? 

30
00:05:34,105 --> 00:05:35,136
<v WOMAN>That’s the one!

31
00:05:35,136 --> 00:05:40,368
<v MAN>Cool. Hey, what’s with all those different coloured boxes outside that place?

32
00:05:40,368 --> 00:05:51,134
<v WOMAN>Oh, that’s for the different materials we recycle. The blue is for metal, the green is for glass and plastics, and the yellow of course is for paper.

33
00:05:51,134 --> 00:05:55,692
<v MAN>Hm, okay. I’ll try and manage to keep all that straight.

34
00:05:55,692 --> 00:05:58,318
<v WOMAN>Oh, no need, they’re each labelled.

35
00:05:58,318 --> 00:06:02,224
<v MAN>Great. So which one would I put magazines in?

36
00:06:02,224 --> 00:06:12,583
<v WOMAN>Actually, they don’t go in any of the bins. Unfortunately, magazines can’t be recycled because of the material they’re made of. It’s such a waste. 

37
00:06:12,583 --> 00:06:15,921
<v WOMAN>So would you be interested in volunteering?

38
00:06:15,921 --> 00:06:20,875
<v MAN>Um, I’ll think about it. Could you send me some more info?

39
00:06:20,875 --> 00:06:39,865
<v WOMAN>Absolutely. Along with the newspaper box, I’ll be sending you our booklet, “ Savvy", that is S-A-double V-Y. It tells you about what you can do to protect the environment in your daily life. Plus it lists things you can do as a volunteer with our group.

40
00:06:39,865 --> 00:06:42,551
<v MAN>Hey, that’s cool, thanks!

41
00:06:42,551 --> 00:06:46,683
<v WOMAN>My pleasure. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

42
00:06:46,683 --> 00:06:48,652
<v MAN>Nope, that’s it!